id: decisions/024-tie-break-basis
title: "Two ladders, named, and the person's explicit choice placed by rule"
kind: decision-frame
record_shape: header
status: draft
authored_by: agent-on-behalf
authored_via: "Claude Code session on Dave's behalf, 2026-09-12; drafted from the JUDGMENTS.md reversal axis 'tie-break-basis'; recommendation is an attributed inference and ratifies nothing"
version: "1.0.0"
updated: 2026-09-12
template_ref: templates/decision.yawn
principal_ref: principal:dave

axis: tie-break-basis
axis_gloss: "when two candidates are otherwise equal, whether the cheaper-to-answer one wins or the one the actor explicitly chose this turn"
poles:
  a: lower-effort-first
  b: explicit-choice-first
split: genuinely-split
draft_status: full
leverage: 12
leverage_formula: "judgments_resolved(8) x split_weight(1) x authored_conflict_bonus(1.5)"

question: >
  When two questions are equally important, does the cheaper one to answer win,
  or the one the person explicitly asked for this turn — and does the executable
  tie-break say the same thing as the prose precedence list three blocks above
  it?

why_it_matters: >
  This is the one place in the tree where the executable rule and the prose
  disagree about who wins — core/inquiry-selection.yawn puts
  explicit_current_turn_choice at highest specificity in preference_precedence,
  fourth in determinism.tie_break below lower_answer_burden, and first in an
  unlabelled considerations list, while the test enforces burden-first and the
  receipt records which rule fired. Once answered, every selector a bot runs
  below Dave inherits one ordering in which the person's explicit choice sits
  where a rule put it, not where a list happened to put it.

state_snapshot:
  description: >
    3 judgments sit at lower-effort-first (2 authored) and 5 at
    explicit-choice-first (1 authored). The repository asserts both directions on
    this axis.
  attributed_to: JUDGMENTS.md
  epistemic_status: observed
  source_refs:
    - JUDGMENTS.md
    - TAXONOMY.md

lacuna:
  kind: unselected_choice
  description: >
    Whether Dave intends the person's explicit choice to beat cheapness among ties
    (which would make B the rule), or only to be visible; the tree records the
    machine's answer, not his.

options:
  - name: A
    description: >
      rule: Lower effort first. determinism.tie_break stands as written
      (hard_gate_priority, greater_semantic_priority, lower_answer_burden,
      explicit_current_turn_choice, accepted_question_order_preference,
      canonical_question_order). preference_precedence is amended to say it orders
      preferences, not candidates. The interaction_fit considerations list is
      reordered to match tie_break or marked `ordered: false`.
    cost: "Two prose edits in core/inquiry-selection.yawn; one added assertion."
    risk: "A person who explicitly asks for a harder question among semantic ties is quietly handed the cheaper one and the only trace is a tie-break line in a receipt."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "Keep tests/orientation-map-v0.1.test.mjs 'lower effort precedes accepted presentation order when semantic priority is tied' and tests/inquiry-selection-receipt-v0.1.test.mjs 'the receipt copies the ranker's actual lower-burden tie-break trace'; add an assertion that an explicit_current_turn_choice for a higher-burden candidate does not beat a lower-burden tied one; a test that any list in core/inquiry-selection.yawn consumed as a rank is labelled priority or tie_break, and considerations is marked unordered."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0250"
      - "J1023"
      - "J1200"
  - name: B
    description: >
      rule: Explicit choice first. When semantic priority ties, the person's
      explicit choice this turn wins before burden is compared: tie_break becomes
      hard_gate_priority, greater_semantic_priority, explicit_current_turn_choice,
      accepted_question_order_preference, lower_answer_burden,
      canonical_question_order. The DeterministicTieBreak ruleOrder const and the
      orientation-map rankingPolicy const change with a policyVersion bump.
    cost: "Edit two schema consts (schemas/inquiry-selection-receipt.v0.1.schema.json ruleOrder, schemas/orientation-map.v0.1.schema.json rankingPolicy), bump policyVersion, regenerate fixture receipts, rewrite two test titles and their assertions."
    risk: "An explicit choice becomes a way to route around burden limits that exist to protect the person (unbounded_or_unnecessary_burden is a hold reason), so the selector must add a burden ceiling the current design gets for free."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "Reverse the two named tests; a new assertion that an explicit current-turn choice among semantic ties beats lower burden; a new assertion that the burden hold still fires above a ceiling regardless of explicit choice."
    evidence_for:
      - "J1012"
      - "J1013"
      - "J1015"
      - "J1016"
      - "J1094"
  - name: C
    description: >
      rule: Two ladders, each named for what it ranks. Semantic ranking keeps effort
      last — what matters is never decided by what is cheap. The deterministic
      tie-break keeps lower_answer_burden before explicit_current_turn_choice, as
      tested. carve-out, written down: an explicit current-turn choice is honoured
      as a presentation choice (order, medium, adapter) and as a reason to hold or
      foreground, never as a rank input, so it can always be seen but never silently
      out-ranks burden. preference_precedence gains one line: it orders preferences,
      not candidates; candidates are ordered by determinism.tie_break. The
      interaction_fit considerations list is marked `ordered: false`.
    cost: "Three prose edits in core/inquiry-selection.yawn; one consistency test; no executable change."
    risk: "Dave in fact wants his explicit choice to win among ties, and C entrenches the machine's preference with a clearer label."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "A test that the three lists in core/inquiry-selection.yawn are consistent with the schema consts (semanticPriorityOrder and ruleOrder) and that any list not labelled priority or tie_break carries ordered: false; keep both existing tie-break tests; a receipt fixture in which an explicit current-turn choice appears under presentation evidence while lower_answer_burden appears as the applied tie-break."
    evidence_for:
      - "J1023"
      - "J1200"
      - "J1012"
      - "J1094"
      - "J0250"
      - "J1013"

recommendation:
  option_ref: C
  confidence: 0.7
  epistemic_status: inferred
  authority: none
  reasoning: >
    By count this is split (3 against 5) and both sides carry authored judgments,
    but by proof it leans hard to lower-effort: J1023 and J1200 are authored and
    proof-attached, and the passing suite implements burden-before-explicit-choice
    in two test files with the receipt copying the trace. On the other side, J1012
    (authored) is about a different ladder — semantic priority, where effort is
    ninth — and does not conflict, since burden enters the tie-break only after
    semantic priority ties; the other four are REVEALED list positions in a block
    the file deliberately calls considerations rather than priority (GROUND_TRUTH
    facts 16 and 18), so the apparent two-cycle is a stated-versus-revealed
    conflict already adjudicated by test. What is genuinely open is J0250:
    preference_precedence puts explicit_current_turn_choice at highest specificity
    and tie_break puts it fourth, and nothing says those are different orderings.
    C writes that down without touching executable behaviour and gives the
    explicit choice a visible place (presentation and hold) instead of a losing
    rank. B is the honest alternative if Dave's intent is that his ask should beat
    cheapness; that is a question only he can answer.

target_condition:
  mode: decide
  proposed_by: agent-on-behalf
  affected_principals:
    - principal:dave
  ratification_status: proposed
  sufficient_when:
    - "core/inquiry-selection.yawn preference_precedence states that it orders preferences, not candidates, and points at determinism.tie_break for candidates."
    - "The interaction_fit considerations list carries ordered: false, or is reordered to match tie_break."
    - "A test in tests/ fails if any of the three lists in core/inquiry-selection.yawn disagrees with the schema consts it is meant to mirror, and passes; both existing tie-break tests stay green."
    - "A receipt fixture shows an explicit current-turn choice recorded under presentation evidence while the applied tie-break is lower_answer_burden."

choice:
  selected_by: ""
  option_ref: ""
  authority_ref: ""

proof:
  covers:
    - target_condition
    - options
  falsifier: >
    A fixture where a person explicitly picks a higher-burden question among
    semantic ties yields a receipt in which lower_answer_burden overrides it, no
    hold or presentation trace shows the choice, and no prose rule can be found
    that says burden wins.
  status: proposed

resolves:
  - "J0250"
  - "J1023"
  - "J1200"
  - "J1012"
  - "J1013"
  - "J1015"
  - "J1016"
  - "J1094"

boundary:
  - "confidence cannot ratify an objective; this record proposes and Dave decides"
  - "the REVEALED judgments cited here describe what artifacts do, not what Dave chose"

source:
  template: templates/decision.yawn
  axis: TAXONOMY.md
  inventory: JUDGMENTS.md
